{"format":1,"seq":119,"writes":[{"type":"entry","value":{"model":[{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Current CAPE sits around 40 versus the 44 peak in December 1999, with Fed funds at 3.88 versus 5.3-6.5 then, and 10-year yields at 5.13 versus 6.28. I should pull more detail on the recent monthly price path, including the max and drawdown, keeping in mind that Yahoo's latest monthly bar for November is still in progress.\n\nI've got the dot-com peaks: SPX monthly close high was August 2000 at 1517.68, with the intraday peak in March 2000 at 1552; Nasdaq's monthly close peak was March 2000 at 4572.83, intraday 5048. Now I'm planning the actual analysis: build monthly SPX and Nasdaq series from 1985 to November 2026, then align the Netscape IPO (August 1995) with ChatGPT's launch (November 2022) as month zero and rebase both to 100, testing different start points in the 1990s window to find the best correlation fit with the current cycle's returns and log levels. I also want to compute cumulative and annualized gains, max drawdown, and volatility for both eras—noting SPX rose roughly 170% from August 1995 to March 2000 versus about 85% now, while Nasdaq surged around 350% then versus 129% in the current run.\n\nI should also chart rolling 5-year CAGR for SPX from 1990 to 2026 as a bubble indicator, since that measure peaked near 28% around 1999, and pull a full CAPE series from multpl.com going back further than Shiller's own data cutoff. Beyond that, I want to compare today's mega-cap leaders' P/E ratios (NVDA, MSFT, AAPL, GOOGL, AMZN, META, AVGO, TSLA) against 2000-era leaders like Cisco, Microsoft, Intel, and Oracle, and compute the excess CAPE yield now versus 1999 using current 10-year Treasury yields.\n\nOn concentration, I don't have reliable top-10 weight data, so I'll skip unsourced figures there. For the analog charts, I'm running into a real constraint: Mermaid's xychart drops any position where a series has a gap, which breaks my plan to overlay the 1990s path continuing past where today's data ends -- and I'm not allowed to hand-write raw Mermaid syntax, so I need another way around this.\n\nI'm weighing whether a Python matplotlib PNG committed to the repo could work instead, giving a richer overlay with peak annotations, versus sticking with chart.xy and splitting it into two separate charts -- one for the overlapping window and one showing the full 1990s path with today's position called out in the caption.\n\n","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_bdrk_01JrdmfCVXywtm5mUyfYC2B9","name":"typescript","arguments":{"code":"// @pit: {\"label\": \"Record data-source facts in session notes and check python/matplotlib and git remote\"}\nasync ({ session: { setNote }, shell: { exec } }) => {\n  await setNote(\"sources\", \"FD/Equibles prices only from 2020/2023. Use Yahoo chart API monthly ^GSPC/^IXIC (period1=473385600, interval=1mo) works. Shiller CSV github datasets/s-and-p-500 PE10 to 2023-09; multpl.com/shiller-pe/table/by-month current CAPE 39.95 (Nov 13 2026), peak Dec1999 44.2. FRED FEDFUNDS Oct2026 3.88 (Dec99 5.30, Jun00 6.53); GS10 Oct2026 5.13 (Dec99 6.28, Jan00 6.66). Today 2026-11-16. SPX Nov2026 7561, Sep 7651.\");\n  const r = await exec(\"python3 -c 'import matplotlib; print(matplotlib.__version__)'; git remote -v; git branch --show-current; cat .gitignore | head -20\");\n  return r as PitJsonValue;\n}"}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"nvidia","model":"aws/anthropic/bedrock-claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":2300,"cacheRead":42986,"cacheWrite":4777,"totalTokens":50065,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0,"totalFromProvider":false},"cacheWrite1h":0,"reasoning":1860},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1794856391716,"responseId":"msg_bdrk_qxw72bopgzkxfiulvjmmxyybtnnxptsu3twpine4sypa7mnn4nva","rawStopReason":"tool_use","durationMs":31304,"thinkingLevel":"high"}],"kind":"pi.assistant","id":43,"conversationId":1,"byTa